course: dictionary
lesson: 9705
title: Words that show a mood
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how a character feels without saying it outright. Sulkily is one of them. It means in a sulky way: quiet, cross and unwilling to be friendly because you are upset or did not get your way.
explain: Read this from Alice in Wonderland: «Well, be off, then!» the Pigeon says sulkily. The Pigeon is not shouting. It sounds quiet and cross, and that tells you it is upset with Alice.
explain: Use the words around sulkily to work out its meaning. Alice has said she does not like raw eggs, and the Pigeon answers in a huffy way, sending her off. Swap in your meaning: does "quietly and crossly" fit the sentence? If it does, you have it.
fact: Sulkily means in a sulky way: quiet, cross and unwilling to be friendly.
fact: You feel sulky when you are upset or did not get your way.
fact: In stories, sulkily tells the reader a character is upset but not shouting.
fact: Toad sulks when he cannot have his way, so a writer might show him speaking sulkily.
fact: «Pettishly» is an old word that means a bit sulkily, so it is a close synonym.
exercise: «Well, be off, then!» the Pigeon says sulkily. What does sulkily tell you about the Pigeon? | *It is upset and cross, and not very friendly | It is shouting at the top of its voice | It is singing a happy song | It is laughing and telling a joke
exercise: In the Alice story an old word, «pettishly», is used. Which word means a bit sulkily? | penitence | composedly | folly | *pettishly
exercise: Toad sulks when he cannot have his way. Why might Toad be sulky? | He is singing a happy song | He has just won a prize | He is very hungry and has not eaten | *He is upset because he did not get what he wanted
test: Sulkily means: | Happily and loudly | Calmly and kindly | *Quiet, cross and unwilling to be friendly | Very fast
test: Which word tells the reader a character is upset but not shouting? | gingerly | hither | rapture | *sulkily
exam: Which is the best meaning of sulkily, and why does it fit the Pigeon? | Calmly, because the Pigeon is at peace | *Crossly and quietly, because the Pigeon is upset with Alice | Loudly, because the Pigeon is shouting at Alice | Happily, because the Pigeon is glad to see Alice
